Feature: A merge that takes none of what it merges never reaches origin

  BL-848's landing merge recorded correct ancestry and an accurate-sounding
  message while taking zero of its second parent's 108 files. The push sweep
  pushed it, the ticket closed, and every audit trail claimed success. These
  scenarios pin the missing question — did the merge actually TAKE anything —
  and, just as importantly, pin that a merge with nothing to take is not a
  fault, since 2 of the 4 tree-equal merges in the last 400 were exactly that.

  Background:
    Given a repository whose history contains merge commits
    And the push sweep is inspecting the commits on main that have not reached origin

  # BL-855 noop-landing-merge-drops-approved-work-01
  Scenario Outline: The verdict turns on whether the merge took what was offered
    Given a merge commit whose second parent offers <offered> paths its first parent lacks
    And that merge commit's tree <tree_relation> its first parent's tree
    When the push sweep decides whether to push it
    Then the push is <verdict>

    Examples:
      | offered | tree_relation   | verdict |
      | 108     | is identical to | refused |
      | 0       | is identical to | allowed |
      | 108     | differs from    | allowed |

  # BL-855 noop-landing-merge-drops-approved-work-02
  Scenario: The refusal states its reason, not a bare status
    Given a merge commit that took none of the 108 paths its second parent offered
    When the push sweep refuses to push it
    Then the refusal names the offending merge commit
    And the refusal names that commit's second parent
    And the refusal states that 108 paths were dropped

  # BL-855 noop-landing-merge-drops-approved-work-03
  Scenario: Being genuinely QA-approved does not excuse a merge that took nothing
    Given a merge commit that took none of the 108 paths its second parent offered
    And that second parent is a genuinely QA-approved commit
    When the push sweep decides whether to push it
    Then the push is refused

  # BL-855 noop-landing-merge-drops-approved-work-04
  Scenario: A dirty working tree cannot change the verdict
    Given a merge commit that took none of the 108 paths its second parent offered
    And the working tree has uncommitted changes to those same paths
    When the push sweep decides whether to push it
    Then the push is refused

  # BL-855 noop-landing-merge-drops-approved-work-05
  Scenario: A non-merge commit is not subject to the check
    Given a commit with a single parent
    When the push sweep decides whether to push it
    Then the push is allowed
